<p>Cuba began restoring electricity after a nationwide grid collapse left about 10 million people without power. As crews worked to bring the system back online, President Trump said the United States would be &quot;doing something with Cuba very soon,&quot; and Secretary of State Marco Rubio called for &quot;new people in charge&quot; on the island. Cuban officials said power had returned across much of the island, though Santiago de Cuba was still offline and authorities warned the aging system could fail again. The outage has intensified a broader confrontation between Washington and Havana as Cuba struggles with deep economic strain, repeated blackouts, and reduced oil supplies. Cuba&#x27;s President Miguel Díaz-Canel answered Trump&#x27;s remarks by vowing &quot;unbreakable resistance&quot; to any takeover attempt, even as separate reports said the two governments are still in talks and some Cubans are urging dialogue over confrontation.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/qGHaBCizir8Y0i66RvIp8eOjnf7KQ8j3uQgpPkbrHVkRSJpOub7AyIJLBH_X8LmVoNd_6qcQMBQBB0kM-X1RjhqKp8eWziAwiZty3A8yCjRTzRD-NSKf9OehI7PH-0PTD3kxrLY9RjK2AJEiiUkyPJAeU9kH-HBOXFw3TUY-ZCpWOV5auanNGkPGCpcI6Lfg1tousgqOcyVfevElztoKKhS6qyZVnwcF9DbrV-QNHwan3b4JJjXFJK6Z8VkSBpl5IQqiyx6eV7TZ8lgkPg' alt='A man charges his phone and his fan with a solar panel during a blackout in Havana on Tuesday. Cuba’s aging grid has drastically eroded in recent years, leading to daily outages.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Recovery was slow: by late Monday, power had returned to about 5% of Havana customers, or roughly 42,000 customers, as hospitals and some homes were gradually reconnected.</li><li>It was the first nationwide grid collapse since the US cut off the island&#x27;s oil supply from Venezuela.</li><li>People familiar with the talks told The New York Times that Washington has said meaningful progress in negotiations requires President Miguel Díaz-Canel to step down.</li><li>The New York Times reported that many Latin American governments, including some on the left, have stayed largely quiet as US pressure on Cuba increases.</li></ul><blockquote>If they decide to try to seize Cuba, they will only find unbreakable resistance there. - Miguel Díaz-Canel</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Cuban government: President Miguel Díaz-Canel accused Washington of inflicting &quot;collective punishment&quot; on Cubans and cast the island&#x27;s crisis as the result of US pressure, not just domestic failures. (<a href='https://www.dw.com/en/cuba-vows-unbreakable-resistance-to-any-takeover-attempt/a-76404114'>DW</a>)</li><li>Trump administration: Secretary of State Marco Rubio said Cuba&#x27;s current leadership cannot solve the country&#x27;s economic problems and called for new leaders. (<a href='https://www.pbs.org/newshour/nation/rubio-calls-for-new-cuban-leaders-as-blackout-underscores-economic-crisis'>PBS NewsHour</a>)</li><li>Cuban residents seeking talks: Some Cubans told reporters they see dialogue with President Trump, not confrontation, as the better path as the crisis deepens. (<a href='https://www.straitstimes.com/world/cubans-clamor-for-dialogue-with-trump-not-confrontation'>The Straits Times</a>)</li><li>US negotiators: The administration is still holding direct talks with Havana rather than shutting them down outright, even as it increases public and private pressure on Cuba&#x27;s leadership. (<a href='https://www.nytimes.com/2026/03/16/world/americas/trump-cuba-president-diaz-canel.html'>The New York Times</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://japantoday.com/category/world/cuba%27s-latest-blackout-underscores-its-deepening-economic-crisis-and-tensions-with-us'>Rubio calls for new Cuban leaders as latest blackout underscores deepening economic crisis</a> - japantoday.com</li><li><a href='https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/mar/17/trumps-explicit-threats-spark-fear-and-loathing-for-struggling-cubans'>Trump’s threats to ‘take’ Cuba signal risi